{"id":"sw-c96adf","type":"COMMENT","payload":{"id":"cmt-4d0109b7-695d-4e14-a7bc-e36e4b6803df","text":"## Summary\n\nGPU timestamp queries around all three passes with EMA-smoothed per-pass timings in the debug overlay.\n\n**New files:**\n- `crates/engine/src/gpu_timing.rs` — `GpuTimestamps` struct: query set, resolve/readback buffers, synchronous `map_async` + `poll(wait_indefinitely)` readback, EMA smoothing (alpha=0.05)\n\n**Modified files:**\n- `crates/engine/src/gpu.rs` — `negotiate_features()` conditionally requests `TIMESTAMP_QUERY`; added `supports_timestamps()` accessor\n- `crates/engine/src/lib.rs` — registered `gpu_timing` module\n- `crates/engine/src/raymarcher.rs` — `render()` now accepts optional `ComputePassTimestampWrites` and `RenderPassTimestampWrites`\n- `crates/viewer/src/main.rs` — creates `GpuTimestamps` when supported, wires timestamp writes to all three passes, reads results at frame start, displays per-pass GPU timings in debug panel\n\n## Rationale\n\nSynchronous readback via `poll(wait_indefinitely())` at frame start. Under VSync the previous frame's GPU work is always complete before the next CPU frame begins, so the poll returns immediately. This avoids async double-buffering complexity for a debug tool.\n\n## Decisions made\n\n- Used `wgpu::PollType::wait_indefinitely()` (wgpu 30's API) rather than the older `Maintain::Wait` — the struct variant requires `submission_index` and `timeout` fields; the convenience constructor gives the \"wait for everything\" behavior.\n- `get_mapped_range()` returns `Result` in wgpu 30 — unwrapped with `expect` since the buffer is guaranteed mapped after `poll(wait_indefinitely)`.\n\n## Status\n\n`cargo clippy` and `cargo test` (14 tests) pass. GPU timing overlay confirmed working on Apple M1 Max / Metal. Ready for review."},"created_at":"2026-08-05T22:32:33.665278Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.222 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"sw-c96adf","type":"ARTIFACT","payload":{"artifact_type":"spec","filename":"spec.md","action":"updated"},"created_at":"2026-08-05T22:43:19.330321Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.222 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"sw-c96adf","type":"COMMENT","payload":{"id":"cmt-c4e1e131-8100-4851-9fc3-97adaa42ce49","text":"## Updated summary (flamegraph added)\n\nOn top of the GPU timestamp queries and debug panel integration, added a flame-chart style frame timeline:\n\n**Frame timeline** (`draw_frame_timeline` in `main.rs`):\n- \"Frame\" egui window with CPU and GPU rows as proportional colored bar segments\n- CPU scopes: Poll GPU, Camera, egui Build, Encode, Submit (measured via `Instant::now()`)\n- GPU scopes: Compute, Blit, egui (from `GpuTimestamps` EMA averages)\n- Both rows share the same time scale; labels on wide segments; hover tooltip with exact ms\n- Profile is one frame behind (previous frame's data displayed in current frame's UI)\n\n**Decision: custom egui widget instead of puffin_egui.**\npuffin_egui 0.30 depends on egui 0.33 — type mismatch with our egui 0.36 context. The custom implementation is ~120 lines, zero dependencies, and gives exactly the two-row CPU/GPU visualization needed.\n\nAll tests pass, clippy clean. Ready for review."},"created_at":"2026-08-05T22:43:29.342985Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.222 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
