{"id":"sw-ac8f44","type":"UPDATE","payload":{"dependencies":[{"source_id":"sw-ac8f44","target_id":"sw-d9a57d","kind":"depends_on"}]},"created_at":"2026-08-06T10:17:15.46349Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.222 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"sw-1e9cf1","type":"CREATE","payload":{"description":"The current shadow path casts a single hard ray along the sun direction. This produces binary shadows with harsh aliased edges that look unrealistic, especially at distance where LOD softens everything else.\n\n**Approach**\n\nReplace the single shadow ray with a **jittered ray** (random offset within a small cone around the sun direction, driven by a per-pixel blue-noise pattern) and **accumulate results across frames** using temporal reprojection:\n\n- Each frame, cast one shadow ray per pixel with a different jitter sample\n- Maintain a screen-space shadow accumulation buffer\n- Reproject the previous frame's accumulated shadow using motion vectors (depth + camera delta)\n- Blend the new sample into the history with an exponential moving average\n- On disocclusion (reprojection fails), reset to the current sample\n\nThis gives N-sample soft shadows at the cost of 1 ray per pixel per frame — the temporal integration does the work that spatial multi-sampling would otherwise require.\n\n**Considerations**\n\n- Blue noise jitter avoids banding artifacts that white noise produces during convergence\n- The jitter cone angle controls penumbra width — could tie this to a \"sun size\" uniform for physically-based penumbra\n- Interaction with the existing `shadows_enabled` toggle: when off, skip the jitter + accumulation entirely\n- Ghosting on fast camera motion: clamp the blend factor or use neighborhood clamping (TAA-style)\n\n**Exit criteria**: shadows visibly soften over 4-8 frames from hard to penumbra; no ghosting on camera motion; no banding artifacts; cost stays at 1 shadow ray per pixel.","estimate":5,"labels":["feature","rendering"],"parent_id":"sw-c13774","sort_order":"ar","status":"PLANNED","title":"Temporal soft shadows: per-pixel jitter + reprojection accumulation"},"created_at":"2026-08-06T10:17:29.643271Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.222 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"sw-6e04b4","type":"CREATE","payload":{"dependencies":[{"kind":"depends_on","source_id":"sw-6e04b4","target_id":"sw-c13774"},{"kind":"depends_on","source_id":"sw-6e04b4","target_id":"sw-7dd08f"}],"description":"The engine is pure compute-shader raymarching — great for static terrain and rigid instances, but unable to perform non-rigid deformation (tree sway, grass bending, leaf rustling, creature animation). Vertex animation requires a mesh representation and a rasterization path.\n\nThis epic introduces a **hybrid renderer**: raymarching remains the primary path for terrain and static props; a lightweight rasterization pipeline handles entities that need animation.\n\n**Motivation**\n\nMGMishMash's dev notes confirm the same conclusion from the mesh side: raymarching is limiting for dynamic content, and a hybrid (RT for static terrain, mesh for animated entities) is the practical end state. Nanite validates compute-shader rendering but still operates on triangles. Vertex hardware is underutilized in our current pipeline.\n\n**Scope**\n\n- Greedy mesh extraction from VoxelModel data (once at load, not per-frame)\n- Vertex animation: wind sway, grass stomping, leaf rustling\n- Hybrid depth compositing between raymarched and rasterized passes\n- Billboard/impostor fallback for distant animated entities\n- Shared lighting: animated entities sample the same AO / GI as terrain\n\n**Dependencies**\n\n- Lighting \u0026 GI (sw-c13774) for shared lighting on mesh entities\n- Atmosphere \u0026 Weather (sw-7dd08f) wind uniforms feed the sway system\n\n**Exit criteria**: trees sway in the wind with per-branch deformation; grass bends away from the player; animated entities cast correct shadows into the raymarched depth buffer; distant vegetation falls back to billboards with no visible pop.","labels":["epic","rendering","animation"],"sort_order":"b0O","status":"BACKLOG","title":"Animation \u0026 Hybrid Rendering: mesh path for animated voxel entities"},"created_at":"2026-08-06T10:17:57.443011Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.222 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"sw-8894f0","type":"CREATE","payload":{"description":"Convert a `VoxelModel`'s brick data into an indexed triangle mesh suitable for rasterization.\n\n**Approach**\n\n- Greedy meshing: merge adjacent same-material faces into larger quads to reduce vertex/triangle count\n- Output per-vertex: position, face normal, palette color, height-above-ground (for wind weighting)\n- Run once at model load time — the mesh is static; animation is applied in the vertex shader\n- Store as a GPU vertex + index buffer alongside the existing VoxelModel brick data\n- Models that are never animated (rocks, pebbles) can skip mesh extraction entirely\n\n**Exit criteria**: a tree VoxelModel produces a triangle mesh that renders identically to its raymarched counterpart when viewed from the same angle; mesh generation completes in \u003c 10ms for a typical tree model.","estimate":5,"labels":["feature","rendering","animation"],"parent_id":"sw-6e04b4","sort_order":"b0P","status":"BACKLOG","title":"Greedy mesh extraction from VoxelModel data"},"created_at":"2026-08-06T10:18:09.047399Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.222 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"sw-2cb83c","type":"CREATE","payload":{"dependencies":[{"kind":"depends_on","source_id":"sw-2cb83c","target_id":"sw-7dd08f"}],"description":"Vertex shader deformation system for animated voxel entities.\n\n**Animation types**\n\n- **Wind sway**: global wind uniform (from Atmosphere epic sw-7dd08f) drives sinusoidal displacement weighted by vertex height-above-ground. Higher vertices sway more. Phase varies by instance world position to avoid synchronized motion.\n- **Grass stomping**: entity positions are passed as a small uniform buffer. Vertices within a radius are pushed radially outward and downward. Prevents visual clipping between creatures and plants.\n- **Leaf rustling**: high-frequency noise displacement on vertices flagged as canopy material. Amplitude modulated by wind strength.\n\n**Considerations**\n\n- Animation parameters are per-material or per-model-tag, not per-vertex bone weights (keeps it simple)\n- The height-above-ground attribute from the mesh extraction step drives sway intensity\n- All animation is procedural in the vertex shader — no keyframe data, no skeleton\n\n**Exit criteria**: trees visibly sway with wind; sway phase varies per instance; grass parts around a moving entity; canopy leaves have subtle high-frequency motion distinct from trunk sway.","estimate":8,"labels":["feature","rendering","animation"],"parent_id":"sw-6e04b4","sort_order":"b0Q","status":"BACKLOG","title":"Vertex animation system: wind sway, grass stomping, leaf rustle"},"created_at":"2026-08-06T10:18:19.104888Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.222 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"sw-0aad35","type":"CREATE","payload":{"dependencies":[{"kind":"depends_on","source_id":"sw-0aad35","target_id":"sw-c13774"}],"description":"Composite the rasterized animated-entity pass with the raymarched terrain/static pass into a single final image.\n\n**Approach**\n\n- Rasterization pass renders animated meshes to a separate color + depth target\n- The blit/composite shader samples both the raymarched storage texture (with its depth) and the rasterized color+depth\n- Per-pixel: pick the closer fragment, output its color\n- The raymarcher already writes depth to a storage buffer for shadow rays — expose this as the terrain depth source\n\n**Considerations**\n\n- Animated entities must also participate in the raymarcher's shadow rays (occlude sunlight). Options: (a) write rasterized depth back into a buffer the shadow ray reads, or (b) accept that animated entities don't self-shadow into the terrain pass (simpler, less correct)\n- Shared lighting: the rasterized pass needs access to the same AO/GI data. The cone-traced AO buffer and light propagation grid should be bindable in the rasterization fragment shader.\n\n**Exit criteria**: a tree mesh renders correctly in front of and behind terrain; no depth fighting at boundaries; animated entities receive the same lighting as raymarched terrain.","estimate":5,"labels":["feature","rendering"],"parent_id":"sw-6e04b4","sort_order":"b0R","status":"BACKLOG","title":"Hybrid depth compositing: rasterized + raymarched passes"},"created_at":"2026-08-06T10:18:28.350616Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.222 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"sw-1bca13","type":"CREATE","payload":{"description":"At low SSE (far distance), swap mesh instances for camera-facing billboard sprites to reduce vertex count.\n\n**Approach**\n\n- Pre-render each VoxelModel from N angles (e.g. 8 or 16 around Y axis, 2-3 elevation tiers) into a texture atlas\n- At runtime, SSE threshold determines mesh vs. billboard (same SSE system as mip LOD selection)\n- Billboard quad uses the atlas slice closest to the current view angle\n- Optionally: animate billboard UVs to approximate sway (shift UV sinusoidally)\n\n**Considerations**\n\n- Atlas generation can run at model load time alongside mesh extraction\n- Billboard depth should be written as a single plane at the instance center — no per-pixel depth, but close enough at distance\n- Transition: cross-fade or dither between mesh and billboard over a small SSE range to avoid pop\n\n**Exit criteria**: distant trees render as billboards; switching from mesh to billboard is not perceptible at the transition distance; vertex count for a forest of 500 trees at distance drops by \u003e 90% compared to full mesh.","estimate":5,"labels":["feature","rendering","perf"],"parent_id":"sw-6e04b4","sort_order":"b0S","status":"BACKLOG","title":"Billboard/impostor fallback for distant animated entities"},"created_at":"2026-08-06T10:18:36.95358Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.222 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"sw-ed6340","type":"COMMENT","payload":{"id":"cmt-ec762b86-79f6-4063-86b4-17769a0db955","text":"\n## Summary\n\nPorted terrain noise generation to a GPU compute shader. 12K cells generate in **86ms** on GPU vs 2534ms on CPU — **29× speedup**.\n\n**New sandbox modules:**\n- `shaders/worldgen.wgsl` — compute shader: direct port of `hash`, `noise3d`, `fbm3d`, `sample_material` from CPU worldgen\n- `gpu_worldgen.rs` — `GpuWorldGenerator`: pipeline setup, batch dispatch (256 bricks/dispatch), staging buffer readback, concurrent results cache\n- `gpu_cached_source.rs` — `GpuCachedSource`: `BrickSource` impl with GPU cache → region disk cache → CPU fallback\n\n**Integration:** Terrain presets now dispatch GPU worldgen before creating the pager. Worker threads pull pre-computed results from the GPU cache (O(1) map lookup) with CPU fallback for any misses.\n\n## Rationale\n\nCPU procgen at ~1ms/brick makes large-world testing impractical (4+ minutes for 262K cells). GPU compute is embarrassingly parallel — 4096 independent noise evaluations per brick map perfectly to a workgroup.\n\n## Decisions\n\n- `smooth` is a WGSL reserved word — renamed to `smoothstep3`\n- Used `device.poll(PollType::wait_indefinitely())` — wgpu 30 API (not `Maintain::Wait`)\n- Blocking readback for `generate_all()` — acceptable during preload; non-blocking tick path can be added later for runtime streaming\n\n## Status\n\n34 tests pass, clippy clean, fmt clean. GPU generates 12288 cells in 86ms on M1 Max. Terrain visually matches CPU output.\n"},"created_at":"2026-08-06T10:19:00.416572Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.222 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"sw-ed6340","type":"ARTIFACT","payload":{"action":"created","artifact_type":"walkthrough","filename":"walkthrough.md"},"created_at":"2026-08-06T10:19:32.049216Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.222 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"sw-ed6340","type":"UPDATE","payload":{"status":"DONE"},"created_at":"2026-08-06T10:19:37.319977Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.222 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"sw-e19362","type":"CREATE","payload":{"assignee":"Claude Code \u003cagent@macbook.local\u003e","dependencies":[{"kind":"depends_on","source_id":"sw-e19362","target_id":"sw-ed6340"}],"description":"\nAdd a 128×16×128 terrain preset (262K cells, 32K pool cap) that forces paging, LRU eviction, and streaming. Includes `preload_radius()` on the pager so only the camera's initial area loads before the first frame — the rest streams in as the camera moves.\n","estimate":2,"labels":["feature","sandbox"],"parent_id":"sw-d6d2c2","sort_order":"a4","status":"DOING","title":"Large World sandbox preset to exercise paging"},"created_at":"2026-08-06T10:20:54.081794Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.222 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"sw-7984f6","type":"CREATE","payload":{"dependencies":[{"kind":"blocks","source_id":"sw-7984f6","target_id":"sw-1e9cf1"}],"description":"Add temporal anti-aliasing to the raymarcher output. This is the foundation for temporal soft shadows (sw-1e9cf1), AO denoising, and the eventual FSR 2.2 integration.\n\n**Approach**\n\n- **Jittered projection**: apply a per-frame sub-pixel offset (Halton 2,3 sequence) to the inverse view-projection matrix before ray generation in the compute shader\n- **Motion vectors**: write per-pixel motion vectors to a storage texture during the raymarch pass. For static geometry this is purely camera-derived (reproject current hit position through the previous frame's view-projection). Object motion vectors come later when animated entities exist.\n- **History buffer**: maintain a previous-frame color + depth buffer\n- **Temporal resolve**: a compute pass that blends the current jittered sample with the reprojected history. Neighborhood clamping (min/max of 3x3 current-frame samples) prevents ghosting. Exponential blend factor ~0.05–0.1.\n\n**Considerations**\n\n- The jittered projection feeds directly into FSR 2.2 later — use the same jitter sequence FSR expects (Halton)\n- Motion vectors become a shared resource: temporal shadows (sw-1e9cf1) and future FSR both consume them\n- The temporal resolve replaces raw output in the blit pass input — the blit still handles the final upscale to display resolution\n- Disocclusion detection: when reprojection lands on a depth discontinuity, fall back to the current sample only\n\n**Exit criteria**: raymarched edges are visually smooth; no ghosting on camera motion; sub-pixel detail (e.g. thin branches) temporally stable; motion vector buffer available for downstream consumers.","estimate":5,"labels":["feature","rendering"],"parent_id":"sw-d6d2c2","sort_order":"as","status":"PLANNED","title":"TAA: jittered projection, motion vectors, temporal accumulation"},"created_at":"2026-08-06T10:22:53.575629Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.222 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"sw-bb6531","type":"CREATE","payload":{"description":"Replace the bilinear blit upscale with AMD FSR 2.2 temporal upscaling. The TAA story provides the prerequisite infrastructure (jittered projection, motion vectors, history buffer); FSR 2.2 replaces the temporal resolve + blit with a single higher-quality pass that both stabilizes and upscales.\n\n**Why this matters**\n\nThe engine renders at 0.5x display resolution and bilinear-upscales. This is the single largest source of visual softness. FSR 2.2 was designed for exactly this case — reconstructing a sharp, temporally stable full-resolution image from a lower-resolution input.\n\n**Approach**\n\n- Port FSR 2.2 algorithm from HLSL/GLSL to WGSL (the algorithm is open source, MIT licensed)\n- Required inputs (all produced by the TAA story): jittered color, depth, motion vectors, exposure\n- Reactive mask: flag pixels without valid motion data (later: particles, transparency, animated entity boundaries)\n- The FSR pass replaces both the TAA temporal resolve and the bilinear blit — one pass does accumulation + upscale\n- Quality modes: Quality (1.5x), Balanced (1.7x), Performance (2.0x), Ultra Performance (3.0x) — maps to our existing render_scale setting\n\n**Spike: WGSL port feasibility**\n\nBefore implementation, assess:\n- Shader complexity and WGSL compatibility (FSR uses wave intrinsics — what's the wgpu fallback?)\n- Input format requirements vs. what our raymarch pass produces\n- Performance on Metal (M1 Pro is the minimum spec target)\n\n**Exit criteria**: half-res raymarched output upscales to display resolution with sharp edges and stable temporal detail; visual quality noticeably exceeds bilinear blit; no regression on M1 Pro frame times vs. current blit.","estimate":8,"labels":["feature","rendering","perf"],"parent_id":"sw-d6d2c2","sort_order":"b0T","status":"BACKLOG","title":"FSR 2.2 temporal upscaling: replace bilinear blit with intelligent reconstruction"},"created_at":"2026-08-06T10:23:05.712739Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.222 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"sw-bb6531","type":"UPDATE","payload":{"status":"PLANNED","dependencies":[{"source_id":"sw-bb6531","target_id":"sw-7984f6","kind":"depends_on"}]},"created_at":"2026-08-06T10:25:30.32763Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.222 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"sw-e19362","type":"COMMENT","payload":{"id":"cmt-0a1d54e1-57de-4f70-a8b8-9db12c5b92f1","text":"\n## Summary\n\nAdded \"Large World\" preset (128×16×128 = 262K cells, 32K pool cap) and `preload_radius()` on the pager.\n\n- GPU gen produces all 262K cells in 1.6s\n- `preload_radius(40m)` loads 14,644 bricks around camera start in 295ms\n- Pool saturates at 32,768 — pager exercises streaming + LRU eviction as camera moves\n- Camera path sweeps 60-90m radius, forcing constant paging\n- 47 FPS avg on M1 Max during streaming benchmark\n\n## Status\n\n34 tests pass, clippy clean, fmt clean.\n"},"created_at":"2026-08-06T10:23:26.503389Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.222 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"sw-e19362","type":"ARTIFACT","payload":{"action":"created","artifact_type":"walkthrough","filename":"walkthrough.md"},"created_at":"2026-08-06T10:23:43.820658Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.222 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"sw-e19362","type":"ARTIFACT","payload":{"artifact_type":"spec","filename":"spec.md","action":"created"},"created_at":"2026-08-06T10:36:20.881579Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.222 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"sw-e19362","type":"COMMENT","payload":{"id":"cmt-1a514f26-d887-45ba-b769-e2853b373aac","text":"\n## Summary\n\nImplemented persistent coarse mip grid, Large World preset (128×16×128), and multiple paging improvements.\n\n**Engine changes:**\n- `coarse_mip_grid.rs` — grid-parallel buffer storing mip levels 2–4 (73 u32/cell). Persists through eviction.\n- `brick_pager.rs` — pre-sorted eviction queue (O(n) once vs O(n) per eviction), worker-side mip computation (off main thread), spatial demand bounds, `preload_radius()`, evicts Resident bricks when no MipOnly available\n- `gpu.rs` — request adapter's actual limits instead of wgpu defaults (128MB → 4GB on M1 Max)\n- `raymarcher.rs` — binding 9 for coarse mip buffer\n- `raymarch.wgsl` — `sample_coarse_mip()` fallback when grid cell is EMPTY, graduated LOD (levels 2/3/4 by SSE)\n\n**Sandbox changes:**\n- Large World preset: 128×16×128 grid, 131K pool, 80m preload radius\n- `GpuWorldGenerator::populate_coarse_mips()` — fills coarse grid for entire world from GPU cache\n- 128 uploads/frame for hitch-free streaming\n\n## Key findings\n\n- wgpu's default `Limits` capped storage buffers at 128MB. The M1 Max supports 4GB. Requesting adapter limits unlocked 131K pool (1GB).\n- Worker-side mip computation eliminated main-thread hitches during upload bursts.\n- Coarse-to-fine generation (not fine-to-coarse) is the correct architecture for km-scale worlds — filed as follow-up epic.\n\n## Status\n\n34 tests pass, clippy clean, fmt clean. Large World renders continuous terrain with coarse mip fallback at distance. Streaming at 128 uploads/frame is hitch-free.\n"},"created_at":"2026-08-06T11:28:58.550697Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.222 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"sw-e19362","type":"ARTIFACT","payload":{"artifact_type":"walkthrough","filename":"walkthrough.md","action":"updated"},"created_at":"2026-08-06T11:29:26.63076Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.222 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"sw-e19362","type":"UPDATE","payload":{"status":"DONE"},"created_at":"2026-08-06T11:29:36.488906Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.222 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"sw-dacdf2","type":"CREATE","payload":{"title":"Coarse-to-fine generation: resolution pyramid for km-scale worlds","description":"\nReplace the current fine-to-coarse pipeline (generate 16³ voxels → compute mips → throw away detail at distance) with a coarse-to-fine approach: generate at the coarsest level everywhere, progressively enhance near the camera.\n\n**Core insight:** noise functions evaluate at any resolution. A level-4 mip (1³) needs ONE sample per brick, not 4096. A 1 km world at coarsest level = 25M bricks × 1 sample = trivial GPU compute. Mountain ranges at the horizon render as single colored blocks — sub-pixel at that distance, no fidelity loss.\n\n**Resolution pyramid:**\n\n| Level | Per-brick | Range | Memory (1 km world) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 4 (1³) | 4 B | entire world | ~100 MB |\n| 3 (2³) | 32 B | ~500m | ~32 MB |\n| 2 (4³) | 256 B | ~200m | ~25 MB |\n| 1 (8³) | 2 KB | ~100m | ~100 MB |\n| 0 (16³) | 5 KB | ~50m | ~100 MB |\n\n**Total: ~350 MB for 1.6 km² with 10 km visibility.** No distance fog cheats.\n\n**Approach:**\n1. On world load: GPU generates levels 3–4 for the entire world (few samples/brick, fast)\n2. As camera moves: GPU generates levels 1–2 for nearby regions\n3. Closest bricks: full 16³ generation (existing GPU worldgen)\n4. Shader DDA checks which resolution is available per cell, reads from appropriate buffer\n5. Each level stored in its own resolution-appropriate buffer — no waste\n\n**Replaces:** the current `CoarseMipGrid` (which is a partial version of this — levels 2–4 for the whole world). The `BrickPool` becomes the level 0–1 cache. The resolution pyramid unifies both.\n\n**Exit criteria:** 5–10 km view distance, continuous terrain from horizon to camera, no fog, sub-second world load, \u003c 500 MB GPU memory.\n","status":"PLANNED","estimate":8,"sort_order":"b0U","dependencies":[{"source_id":"sw-dacdf2","target_id":"sw-d6d2c2","kind":"relates_to"},{"source_id":"sw-dacdf2","target_id":"sw-e19362","kind":"depends_on"}],"labels":["epic","rendering","perf"]},"created_at":"2026-08-06T11:29:58.398628Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.222 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"sw-625b6d","type":"CREATE","payload":{"title":"Direct coarse worldgen: generate mip levels 2-4 from noise without full 16³","description":"\nGPU compute shader that generates coarse mip data (levels 2-4) directly by evaluating noise at sub-block centers. Currently we generate full 16³ voxels (4096 samples) then average down to mips. For distant terrain, we only need 1-64 noise samples per brick.\n\nThis is the unlock for km-scale worlds: 25M bricks × 1 sample vs 25M × 4096.\n","status":"DOING","parent_id":"sw-dacdf2","estimate":3,"sort_order":"a4","assignee":"Claude Code \u003cagent@macbook.local\u003e","labels":["feature","sandbox","perf"]},"created_at":"2026-08-06T11:49:19.698872Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.222 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"sw-625b6d","type":"ARTIFACT","payload":{"artifact_type":"spec","filename":"spec.md","action":"created"},"created_at":"2026-08-06T11:50:08.036594Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.222 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"sw-625b6d","type":"COMMENT","payload":{"id":"cmt-acc4cd7d-830d-40a0-9782-299104226700","text":"\n## Summary\n\nGPU compute shader generates coarse mip levels 2–4 directly from noise at sub-block centers (1–64 samples per brick) instead of generating full 16³ voxels (4096 samples) then averaging down.\n\n- `worldgen_coarse.wgsl`: 73 threads per workgroup, each evaluates noise at its sub-block center\n- `GpuWorldGenerator::generate_coarse()`: dispatches coarse shader, writes directly to CoarseMipGrid\n- `GpuWorldGenerator::generate_near()`: scoped full 16³ gen for nearby bricks only\n- Cave noise included in coarse gen (initial version skipped it — caves are multi-brick features, not sub-brick detail)\n- Small presets (Default, TerrainOnly) use full gen path; Large World uses coarse + near\n\n## Timing (262K cells, M1 Max)\n\n| Step | Before | After |\n|---|---|---|\n| World coarse mips | 2152 ms | **307 ms** (7×) |\n| Near full gen (122K) | — | 632 ms |\n| Preload (68K) | 9504 ms | 2108 ms |\n\n## Status\n\n34 tests pass, clippy clean, fmt clean.\n"},"created_at":"2026-08-06T12:14:14.770566Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.222 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
