{"id":"sw-a6a691","type":"COMMENT","payload":{"id":"cmt-e4c8726d-dd69-412b-a7b6-a09a298cffaa","text":"\nAttempted three approaches, all failed:\n\n1. **Color blending at brick edges** — smears materials (grass into stone, water into dirt)\n2. **Occupancy-gradient normals** — noisy, creates splotchy lighting\n3. **Fixed up-normal for mip hits** — speckle on vertical/side-facing surfaces\n\nThe root issue: per-brick mip rendering treats each brick as an independent box. You can't smooth across boxes without either smearing materials or breaking lighting on non-horizontal surfaces.\n\n**Blocked on sw-dacdf2** (coarse-to-fine resolution pyramid). The proper fix is rendering distant terrain from a continuous coarse representation rather than per-brick boxes. The resolution pyramid naturally provides smooth transitions because each mip level covers multiple bricks as a single continuous volume.\n\nReverted shader to pre-blending state.\n"},"created_at":"2026-08-06T12:40:58.891361Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.222 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
